# config-schema.waivers — the config-stability gate's ONLY escape hatch (busbar 1.5.3, unit F).
#
# The config grammar is FROZEN after 1.5.3: additive-only forever (new OPTIONAL key / new section /
# new enum variant). `scripts/config-stability-gate.sh` fails RED on anything else. This file is the
# narrow, auditable way to say "yes, this specific break is intentional and reviewed".
#
# WHY A FILE AND NOT AN ENV VAR: an env var can be set in a workflow edit and reviewed by nobody. A
# waiver here is a line in the PR diff, sitting right next to the break it excuses, and a reviewer
# sees it. The gate also prints every applied waiver loudly in its output.
#
# RULES (enforced by scripts/config-schema.py load_waivers + proven by the gate's self-test):
#   - One waiver per line:   <exact-path> = <reason>
#     paths are  Type.field  |  Type::Variant  |  Type  |  "type AliasName"
#   - EXACT paths only. No globs, no wildcards — `*` or `?` is a hard error. A waiver excuses ONE
#     path and nothing else; any other break in the same change is still RED.
#   - A reason is MANDATORY. `path =` with no text is a hard error.
#   - A STALE waiver (one that no longer matches any break) is itself a hard error, so this file can
#     never accumulate standing permission to break something later.
#
# A waiver is a LAST RESORT. The additive path — add a NEW optional key and deprecate the old one —
# is almost always available and is what 1.5.3 froze the grammar to guarantee. If you are adding a
# line here, the change needs a human decision, not a config edit.
#
# EMPTY BY DEFAULT. It should stay that way.

# ── 1.6.0 UNIFIED POOLS (design: 1.6.0-unified-pools.md, owner "GO", 2026-08-20) ─────────────────
# The three failover config grammars collapse into ONE neutral top-level `pools:`. Two deliberate,
# owner-ratified deltas the additive gate flags — both ZERO operator impact:
#
# 1. `tool_pools:`/`agent_pools:` are UNRELEASED 1.6.0-dev sections (latest tag is v1.5.4, which never
#    had them). They fold into the single neutral `pools:` map (kind inferred from members). Removing
#    an unreleased section breaks no shipped config.
DeployCfg.tool_pools = 1.6.0 unified pools: unreleased 1.6.0-dev section folded into the neutral `pools:` map (kind inferred). Zero operator impact; the migrator carries it.
DeployCfg.agent_pools = 1.6.0 unified pools: unreleased 1.6.0-dev section folded into the neutral `pools:` map (kind inferred). Zero operator impact; the migrator carries it.
#
# 2. `PoolMember` gained a hand-written Deserialize that accepts a BARE NAME on every plane OR the
#    legacy rich object. The rich object's fields now live on the `RichMember` shape the visitor
#    forwards a map to (see the `additive OK RichMember` / `RawPoolCfg.*` lines), so EVERY former
#    `PoolMember.*` key is still accepted with the same type — the grammar is a SUPERSET, not a break.
#    The generator only sees the fields relocated off the now-manual `PoolMember` type.
PoolMember.model = 1.6.0 unified pools: still accepted via RichMember (map arm of the new PoolMember visitor); grammar is a superset (also accepts a bare name).
PoolMember.weight = 1.6.0 unified pools: still accepted via RichMember; grammar is a superset (bare name or rich object).
PoolMember.context_max = 1.6.0 unified pools: still accepted via RichMember; grammar is a superset (bare name or rich object).
PoolMember.tier = 1.6.0 unified pools: still accepted via RichMember; grammar is a superset (bare name or rich object).
PoolMember.attempt_timeout_ms = 1.6.0 unified pools: still accepted via RichMember; grammar is a superset (bare name or rich object).
PoolMember.reasoning = 1.6.0 unified pools: still accepted via RichMember; grammar is a superset (bare name or rich object).
PoolMember.tags = 1.6.0 unified pools: still accepted via RichMember; grammar is a superset (bare name or rich object).
